welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is a DITA boyanov ska she is professor  of Slavic languages and literature and  chair of the European studies counsel at  the Macmillan Center professor buljanoff  ska studies and teaches about 19th  century russian literature and  intellectual history as well as Empire  and nation in Russian culture today  we'll talk with Professor bouillon Alka  about her new book a world of empires  the Russian voyage of the frigate pelota  welcome professor boyanov ska thank you  for having me let's start with an  overview of your book tell us about it  so my book is about a famous mid 19th  century Russian voyage around Africa and  Asia to open up Japan to European trade  now most people who have heard of this  part of history think of the u.s.  Commodore Matthew Perry because he's  typically credited with opening up Japan  to European trade but the Russian  expedition that I write about was  actually following Perry very closely  and there arrived in Japan roughly three  weeks three weeks after the Americans  and they actually they both negotiated  with the Japanese at roughly the same  time so you might also wonder well  what's the big deal about signing a  treaty with another country it so  happens that in the mid 19th century  Japan the Government of Japan forced  swore any contacts with Western European  powers so this was an attempt by the  American and Russian missions to force  Japan to open to Western trade to open  its treaty its its ports and its economy  and to structure that trade relationship  in a way that benefited Western powers  now before the Russian expedition  reached Japan they sailed alongside the  western shores of Africa they made a  month-long stop in the Cape Colony so  today's South Africa they crossed the  Indian Ocean and Mae stops in the ports  of  Manila Singapore Hong Kong Shanghai and  then sailed alongside the northern  shores of Korea so there was this was  really an incredible incredible voyage  and luckily we have among the crew of  that expedition a famous Russian writer  even Goncharov who happened to write a  book about it a travelogue and it is  titled the frigate pelada after the  expedition's flagship okay so and it  also describes actually the gunshot offs  overland journey through Siberia back to  European Russia so I take this mid 19th  century Russian travelogue and Probate  for insights about Russia's Imperial  identity but also use it as a lens on to  mid 19th century global colonial history  so it's not just a book about Russians  but about Russians on a global stage so  readers of my book will get snapshots of  Cape Town of Singapore of Hong Kong of  Shanghai of Nagasaki and also unicoos  which was the capital of Russian Siberia  at the time okay  fascinating so a couple of questions one  off of the bat is what led you to write  the book did you come across goncharov  chronicle and you got interested that  way what was the impetus so I actually  stumbled upon this project I read one  day gunshot offs biography and I found  out that he made us incredible voyage  and then he wrote a travelogue about it  and I figured given the locations where  the voyage took him surely he'll have  something to say about the kind of  imperial questions that I was very  interested in pursuing that's it I  didn't plan to make it into a book it  was supposed to be a chapter in my other  bigger project it's called the Empire in  the Russian classics  but as I kept working on it and and and  digging in all those issues its sort of  new nodes kept proliferating in all  directions and to really attend to them  was no longer possible with the confines  of a single chapter so I decided to make  a separate book about it okay so and how  other than reading the book did you do  additional research around it I wish I  had a more colorful answer to that  question but it actually writing this  book meant going to the library a lot  and reading lots of books I was spending  a fellowship year at the time at the  Institute for Advanced Study in  Princeton which is you know as close to  scholars paradise as you can possibly  imagine and I had a chance to speak with  a lot of scholars who knew the histories  of the various places that I needed to  write about and they health with by  suggesting books and other reading  material so now thinking back I really  really wish that you know that I had  written an amazing grant proposal that  would justify my travel to all those her  fabulous places so I guess that would  have meant postponing the project which  but I didn't really really didn't want  to do this at the same time the books of  the various scholars I've read  historians of Britain of South Africa of  China of Japan of Siberia did give me  amazing  armchair Germany said the least that  said I I have a lot of ideas about  potential vacation locations which is  all the you know all the more old a  nicer in that I actually do know now a  little bit about the histories of those  places if I ever go there right yeah how  long was the his voyage they set out in  the fall of 1852 and then they arrived  in Japan early 1853 it's a little  complicated but at a certain point  actually the tsunami  destroyed the Russian ship oh my  goodness so there's there's a lot of  dramatic turns in the book but the  mission itself continued until 1853 then  the Russian sent a new friendly frigate  and the the diplomacy and negotiations  with Japan continued until 1855 I  believe at the same time gunshot off my  writer did not stick around to see that  happen because he was getting a little  tired of traveling and his wanderlust  was sated so he decided to return  overland back to Russia which is why he  took that very very arduous journey  across Siberia he actually called it  that he said that no drama and storms on  the hide he sees matched the sort of  adversities of handling Siberian rivers  and Siberian mountains and oh and the  Siberian muds  you know swamps I would imagine the cold  too was yes any surprises while you're  you know reading the book something that  you didn't perhaps expect yeah I guess I  didn't surprise I didn't expect to see  to open this mid 19th century Russian  book and to basically see a vision of  the world that I know so well from my  life now and this is also something that  was actually the biggest surprised for  gunshot off my author and what I mean by  that is that the degree to which the  world of 1850s Imperial frontiers was  interrelated the sort of accelerating  forces of globalization that he came to  observe in these various ports of call  was really something that struck him and  he he he was a very keen observer of of  that new kind of globe more globalized  world coming into being so  I guess what has surprised me is also  the same thing that surprised him and  interestingly there is a very excellent  recent biography by Joseph Joseph Conrad  by myosin off which stresses Conrad's  presence in foreseeing the forces of  globalization at the end of the 19th  century but here with gunshot often the  frigate pelada I have you know a writer  and a book that beats Conrad to it by  half a century so that was truly truly  amazing for me to learn so what are some  of your insights about what Goncharov  writes about Russia's foray into  globalization this book is a very rich  document of Russia's Imperial worldview  in the 19th century that resonated very  broadly with the Russian public's this  book presented very powerful arguments  that Russia must get more engaged in  imperial expansion and colonial activity  in securing new markets in getting more  active in international trade in so in  that sense it was a sort of a an  argument for Russia stepping more  decisively into the global arena  well peopled by representative of all  these other empires so and that is the  message that Goncharov presented  exceedingly well other ways in which  this book resonated very well with  Russian leaders is that it combined very  nicely a sense that Ganser office was a  self-confident European on all these  imperial frontiers but at the same time  viewed these worlds through a  distinctively Russian lens so that a lot  of reviewers commented on that on that  very special quality of the book so so  yes these are some of the some of the  highlights for their readers in Russia  at the time of this particular book but  the  important thing to remember about is  that it wasn't a sort of just a  description of places it was really a  book with arguments right about Russia  becoming more competitive in Imperial  enterprises and sort of catching up with  with other empires who were who were  present and active in imperial frontiers  in Asia and Africa sure sure so let's  talk about travel logs and and were they  very popular at this time and and how  was the frigate pelota received in  Russia mm-hmm  travel logs were incredibly popular in  the 19th century so you know travel  writing of explorers such as Stanley  Livingston were some of the biggest  bestsellers of the 19th century Mark  Twain is actually better now well it was  better known in the 19th century to  American readers as an author of travel  as the author of traveler books not of a  novelist the public collection for the  monument of Nia of nikolai przhevalsky a  famous russian explorer of central asia  actually rivaled the contributions that  were given to the monument of Russia's  national poet Alexander Pushkin so  excerpts from travel books were  everywhere in journals in newspapers it  was everyone's favorite reading because  travel books really combined in a very  attractive whole colorful information  and useful information about the world  but also colorful narrative about it  right so you had a little bit of  adventure and some you know lyrical  descriptions of sunsets and some  colorful you know episodes about  interactions with the local people often  imbued with humor but at the same time  you got your basic information about the  history politics a culture  economy anthropology ethnography of  various of these various new places and  if you were Charles Darwin lots of  natural history right so it was a sort  of in one pill you were to get all these  different types of content so so that's  why these books were so popular and  gunshot offs travelogue is no exception  it was actually an Imperial era  bestseller  even though he's best known as a  novelist in Russia this book outsold any  of the novels that he wrote it was  incredibly popular it was read by young  adult readers and women and government  officials it was part of military  Academy's curricula and also taught in  regular schools the government actually  promoted it as the model for all travel  writing at least for people that it  employed to write so so this is really a  book that you know if you're interested  in connections between literature and  society this is a book for you and what  do you conclude in your book and what  would you like people to take away from  it if it's just one thing what we let's  see well if we think of modern imperial  modern European imperialism what comes  to mind well it might be Britain and  it's Indian colonies or French in  Algeria or maybe the Spanish colonies in  South America right Russia typically  would not come up in this lineup so my  one takeaway from meters the thing that  I really hope they take from this book  is that Russia should be part of this  story it is a country that in its guise  as a modern Empire was second in size  only to the British  and it is as a result of the Imperial  processes that it contained that it is  at this point the biggest country on  earth so what I'm hoping is that when we  think of European imperialism and  colonialism Russia will become part of  this history and part of this story and  I would also hope that reading my book  readers might would actually think that  it would be it is fascinating to  actually think of Russia and to study  Russia in in this aspect okay  this has been very interesting thank you  for being here with us today and sharing  your work thank you for more information  about professor bouillon Houska and her  research please visit our website at  macmillan report Yale edu be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale thank you so much  you
 

